+## Embedding (feature `embed`)
+
+A page can show a board inside its own interface and keep the file itself (a wiki, a notes application). Build the engine with the
+`embed` feature, and usually without the network of shared sessions (feature `net`, on by default): an embedded board never joins a
+session, and the build then leaves the network out:
+
+```bash
+cargo build --release -p inkboard-app --bin inkboard --target wasm32-unknown-unknown --no-default-features --features web-glow,embed
+wasm-bindgen --target web --no-typescript --out-name inkboard --out-dir <folder> target/wasm32-unknown-unknown/release/inkboard.wasm
+```
+
+Optionally run `wasm-opt -Os` on `<folder>/inkboard_bg.wasm`, as Trunk does for the page of its own. Serve
+`crates/inkboard-app/assets/icons.bin` **next to the host page**: the engine fetches `icons.bin` relative to the page's address the first
+time the icon library is opened. Starting the module installs the API instead of the page of its own:
+
+```js
+import init from './inkboard.js';
+await init();                                  // runs the wasm `main`, which sets globalThis.inkboardEmbed
+const board = await inkboardEmbed.mount(canvas, {
+  board: text,                                 // the native file's text; null: a new empty board
+  name: 'Plan',                                // the board's name (file names of the exports)
+  theme: 'light',                              // 'light' | 'dark' | 'board' (default): the interface only, never the board
+  lang: 'en',                                  // 'en' | 'fr'; default: the browser's language
+  readOnly: false,
+  onReady() {},                                // the first frame is drawn
+  onChange({ revision }) {},                   // the board or the text being typed changed
+  onSave() {},                                 // Ctrl/Cmd+S: the host saves
+  onError({ kind, message }) {},               // kind 'panic': the engine stopped
+});
+```
+
+`inkboardEmbed.version` is the package version followed by `+embed`, and `inkboardEmbed.formatVersion` the version of the native format
+the engine reads and writes. `mount` rejects with `{kind, message}`: `webgl` (no WebGL 2), `load` (the board cannot be read) or `start`
+(the engine could not start, or another board is mounted). The handle:
+
+| Call | What it does |
+|---|---|
+| `snapshot()` → `{board, revision}` | the board in the native format, the text being typed included, unused images left out; synchronous, and it works in a hidden tab |
+| `setBoard(text, name?)` | shows another board (`null`: a new empty one) with a new history and nothing left of the previous board's editing; an unreadable text throws `{kind: 'load', message}` and the current board stays |
+| `revision()`, `isDirty()`, `markSaved(revision)` | the revision counts the changes: after saving the snapshot of revision *r*, call `markSaved(r)` |
+| `setTheme(theme)`, `setReadOnly(on)`, `setName(name)` | `setName` adds no undo step and changes no revision |
+| `setActive(on)` | `false` while the host page hides the board: no periodic repaint; `true` repaints at once |
+| `focus()`, `fitView()` | |
+| `destroy()` | stops the engine; the handle then does nothing, and `mount` may be called again |
+
+- **One board per page.** The browser side keeps one application state, so a second `mount` rejects with `start` ("already mounted")
+  until the first board is destroyed. A host that shows several boards swaps them with `setBoard`, or gives each its own `<iframe>`.
+- **Changes:** `onChange` comes at most once per frame and never in the middle of a gesture. While text is being typed, `isDirty()` stays
+  true even after `markSaved`: the text becomes part of the board when the editing ends, which is reported as a change.
+- **What the host page owns is not shown:** the menu has no New, Open, Join, Save, Save as, Language or Quit, no "Present full screen"
+  (F5 does nothing), and no "Animate the agent"; the title card has no board name and no "Saved" label; the tab title is left alone.
+  The board reads neither the page's address (invitation links) nor the network events, and it takes pastes only while it has the focus.
+- Callbacks are called after the frame that caused them, never from inside it, so they may call the handle.
+
